Well, here we are on the bleeding edge once again.  I would have thought
that they would have ESDI all figured out (pass'e now that SCSI's about),
but apparently not.

We've purchased an Emulex QD25 ESDI controller (one of their newest and
finest?).  We are trying to hang two WREN VI ESDI drives on the controller,
but have experienced limited success.  The drives appear to VMS 5.4-2
on our uV 3400 as $101$DUA3: and $101$DUA4: (yes, we have a LAVc
with ALLOCLASS = 101) as expected.

When we install new controllers and disks, we exercise them with a home
grown exerciser.  The exerciser operates on a single foreign mounted drive.
Once the exerciser is started, it makes an initial pass, writing 2000 blocks
randomly over the disk.  The content of each block written is itself random,
and is saved for a later compare operation.  (Yes, there is logic in the
exerciser to prevent writing to the same block more than once before the
compare operation is performed.)  With the initial pass complete, the
exerciser reads a block previously written and compares the content read
to that which was written.  Following the compare, another random block is
written.  Repeat ad nauseam.  Of course, error checking is performed and
reported (otherwise, what's the point?).  Nothing spectacular here.

What is of interest, are the errors reported by the exerciser for the QD25.
After a sometimes lengthy period of exercise, a drive will fall offline:

  %SYSTEM-F-MEDOFL, medium is offline

If two processes are exercising a drive each, the remaining drive posts:

  %SYSTEM-F-INCSEGTRA, incomplete segmented transfer request posted

sometimes several times, and then continues exercising.

So the first question should be obvious: Has anybody using
the QD25 experienced drives falling offline?

The second question has to do with the drive select lights on the WREN VI's.
The two drives that I'm attempting to hang off the QD25 were at one time
hung off a Dilog DQ686 ESDI controller.  On that controller, the drive
select lights lit only when one would think they should (power on,
spin up, and performing an I/O operation).  When attached to the QD25,
the drive select lights remain lit following any I/O operation.  With
both drives attached and idle, the other drive gets an occasional select
(the other light blips).  I can fathom why the designers might have left
the most recently used drive selected (for performance reasons),
but is this really the case on other QD25s?

All defaults for the QD25 were selected, with the exception of the
controller address.  Emulex technical support had me enable the Autotune
feature, but to no avail.  The controller firmware is at Rev Level C.
Any help is greatly appreciated.

