In article <9106240507.AA26645@cwns2.INS.CWRU.Edu>,
 am728@cleveland.Freenet.Edu (Randy L. Betke) writes:
> I've been trying to install a DHV11 serial card in my MicroVAX II, but
> haven't had any luck.  I've gone into sysgen, and configured device
> DHV11, but when I do a 'show devices' in VMS, it's not there.  

When using SYSGEN's CONFIGURE command, you need to tell it about *all* of the
devices in your bus.  The vector and CSR of the DHV are in "floating vector"
and "floating CSR" space, which means that their values depend in part on the
presence or absence of other devices before the DHV (that's "before" in terms
of the order that things appear in floating CSR space, not in terms of physical
order on the bus), and the presence of the DHV may in turn affect the addresses
of other things in your system.  

In particular... if you have more than one MSCP disk controller and/or more 
than one TMSCP tape controller in your machine, the DHV11 gets configured 
differently than it does if you have just one of each.  (But going from no
tapes to one tape doesn't affect the DHV.  What fun!) 

Here is the recommended procedure for adding a Q-bus option:

0.  Take it out of your system if you haven't done so already, and reboot.

1.  Do SYSGEN> SHOW/CONFIG to get a list of what you currently have. 

2.  Do SYSGEN> CONFIG .  At successive CONFIG> prompts, list all of your
current devices.  ^Z to exit.  Save the resulting list.  

2a. Verify that the CONFIG output (what you told CONFIG that you have) matches
the SHOW/CONFIG output (which is what SYSGEN AUTOCONFIGURE found on your last
boot).  If it doesn't, you missed putting a device into the CONFIG prompts, or
some such.  Correct the problem before proceeding.  Exception:  The first MSCP
disk controller's vector as shown by CONFIG will not match the SHOW/CONFIG
output; don't worry about it. 

3.  Repeat step 2, this time adding the new option to the list.  Compare
this output with that previously obtained.  Note any changes in CSR and vector
assignments to existing devices, as well as the required settings for the new
one.  

4.  Shut down.  Make the indicated changes in ALL options.  Reboot.  

If your new option still doesn't show up, you may be configuring it wrong. You
can check the CSR setting... sort of... with a SYSGEN SHOW/UNIBUS command. This
probes Q-bus CSR space (even though the command says SHOW/UNIBUS) for all
responding addresses and shows the results.  In theory it is a little dangerous
to use because some older options have CSRs that work on a "destructive read"
basis, which is to say that if you get in and read something that same data is
no longer available for the device's driver to read.  In practice, I have been
doing SHOW/UNIBUS commands on live systems for years and have yet to see a
problem, but there's always a first time... Anyway, try this with and without
your new option installed.  With the new option installed you should see a few
more lines of output from SHOW/UNIBUS, and the octal address on one of these
lines should correspond with the intended CSR address of your new option.  If
you don't see this, look carefully for other changes.  Your option may be at
the wrong address, and the wrong address may or may not overlap with some 
other option's.  

Note that most devices respond at more than one address. The "CSR address" that
you set is generally (but not always) the lowest address of the lot. 

> Is the driver for a DHV11 called YFDRIVER or TXDRIVER?  

YFDRIVER is the port driver for DH-class devices.  The class driver for all 
terminal devices is TTDRIVER.  TXDRIVER no longer exists, at least not under 
Version 5.  Sorry, but I don't have a V4 system handy to look at.  

	--- Jamie Hanrahan, Kernel Mode Consulting, San Diego CA
Chair, VMS Internals Working Group, U.S. DECUS VAX Systems SIG 
