Subject: The VT103/II uVAX terminal story.
Message-ID: <7755.288d2374@me.chalmers.se>
Date: 24 Jul 91 05:51:32 GMT
Organization: Dep. of Mechanical Engineering, Chalmers , Sweden.
Lines: 56

Hi NET.
Some of you might remember a small discussion about rebuilding the VT103
terminal from DEC into a small VAX computer. It is possible to do this.
With the help of DEC's wonderful paperbacks from the past, interested
people of the net and a certain Ms Eichert I mannaged to rebuild this
interesting piece of equipemnt. But first, what on EARTH is a VT103?!?
Well, in the golden 16 bit pre workstation days people thought it would
be nice to have a table top computing muscle. So various intelligent terminals
were launched by uncle Digital to make them happy. Mostly rather fixed things
without backplane built around the LSI11-II (or the dual PDP11/03 as some
might know it). This was a old and slow and charming CPU with 16 bit adress
range. It was OK for RT11 and the like though. The machines without backplanes
was called PDT11/130 if housed in a VT100 terminal or PDT11/150 if bigger
stand alone computers with floppies and several serial lines. There was a
machine called VT103 however. It was a standard VT100 with an 18 bit ZIG-ZAG
quad backplane inside... Now THAT was good news. It ment you could shove in
a PDP11/23 or bigger and run multi user ON YOUR TERMINAL! The backplane was
not suited for machines with 22 bit adress range and could not be rebuilt. Or
could it?  Close examination of the backplane shows this almost to good to be
true construction. The backplane is a wire wrap backplane SOLDERED to a
printed cirquit board at the very end of the wire wrap pins. Desolder it,
shorten the pins, wire what you like and presto! I like to think that
somebody nice at DIGITAL thought of the little tinker fellow out there, but
probably the plane turned out this way because it was the cheapest way to do 
it. I turned to the NET and to the litterature of Golden DECs from the past
(supermicrocomputer handbooks :) ). A person from out there, Ms Eichert,
had the same idea as I and together we mannaged to track down info on how
to do it. The backplane has eight dual slots in a ZIG-ZAG pattern. We
made the first two rows into Q22-CD slots and the rest into Q22 in ZIG-ZAG.
For those who do not know what this is, it means that with a little handiwork
(MUCH handiwork, to be honest...) one could turn this old heap of nostalgia
into a carrier of more modern CPU/MEM and periferals.
I did the rebuilding and many told me how it would fry. It did not, but
I had just a PDP11/23+ CPU to test with. I was then told that it would
fry if I put in something bigger and that the power was not clean enough
to supply food to faster brains. A PDP/11-73 CPU popped in and worked like
a charm. Finally a MikroVAXII CPU and mem went my way and I reluctantly 
installed it in my VT103/II and it booted right up.
I do not want to push things so I have had it running for about a week as most
but it seems to live on inside the old VT103. I have this VT103 as my
"traveling VAX" when I need something mobile and powerful. I also use to
tell them INTEL fellows what I think is a good personal computer. It is
not as fast as a 386 or 486 or whatever they are called, but it runs VMS or
ULTRIX. :)
I guess this tells us a little about the sturdiness and quality of the old
DEC stuff that hangs around down at the lab or in the far corner of the
computer room.. 
Now for the legal part of it. I think your license goes with your CPU number
and not with your backplane. I think it is OK to do a stunt like this
provided you have a license for your CPU board but I am not sure.
Could anyone at DIGITAL tell us?
Finally: I have these peripherals inside:
CPU, 8 MEG RAM board, DZQ11, four line async multiplexer and an RQDX3 and
external disk. Just hook up four VT100 to the little can, shuffle them
and let people guess which one is the computer. :)
/L. Persson, Harlosa PD computer centre.


