One of my co-workers just read to me over the phone a paragraph from the
July 22 issue of Network World.  It says that DEC is eliminating OSI
capabilities from DECnet Phase V.  Instead, Phase V will support TCP/IP,
and OSI support will be offered as a separate product called Advantage
Networking.  This is the first we've heard of this here; did we miss a
major announcement, or has Network World leaked something?  Our local
DEC salescritters don't seem to have a straight answer.

Several questions:

1.  Does anyone know if this is really true?

2.  What happens to the OSI support that they were already working on
for Ultrix?

3.  If it is true, does anyone know what this Advantage software will
cost for: (1) a low-end Decstation, or (2) a Vax 6000-series?

Please don't send me TCP/IP vs. OSI flames.  I have an application
where OSI support is mandatory.

