Organization: Polytechnic of Central London
Lines: 43

A group loosely associated with us uses a PDP-11 (not sure what flavour)
running RT-11 for some esoteric data capture.  They now want to be able
to transfer the data to a Sun 3/50 for manipulation, and they've come to
us for suggestions on how best to do this.  I know next to nothing about
RT-11, so I need help.

The requirement is to regularly (about once a week?) transfer about 35MB
of data.  As things stand, I think the quickest way would be to dump the
data to 9-track magtape, walk it to our main site, transfer it to 60MB
cartridge, walk it back, and read the cartridge on the Sun.  Obviously
I'd prefer to come up with a solution they can handle on their own.
They, of course, want to spend no money.

1.  Kermit.  It'd be painfully slow for this volume of data, but they want
    to try it anyway.  But before I can get Kermit onto the machine I need
    to know what magtape format RT-11 uses.

2.  Physical transfer of the disk.  The PDP's disk apparently has an ST506
    interface, and they have an Adaptec 4000 adaptor they can use to get it
    talking to the Sun's SCSI.  I can provide them with code to read the
    disk on a sector-by-sector basis, but obviously it'd help if I knew
    the RT-11 volume format!  I'm not too keen on this idea, because the
    repeated matings of the connectors would eventually give rise to problems.

3.  Parallel comms.  From memory, it seems to me there was something called
    a DR-11.  We could possibly scrounge one of these from somewhere; then
    with the aid of a PC with a parallel card and an ethernet card (which
    we could probably lend them) they've got an indirect route to transfer
    the stuff. It'd need programming on both DOS and RT-11, of course.

4.  Ethernet.  Any chance of RT-11 driving a DEUNA?

There you have it.  We don't have the staff resources to get too deeply
involved in this ourselves, but I don't mind spending a few days coding.
What I need is a pointer to the disk and tape formats, and ideas.  Anyone?

Regards,
Malcolm
