In article <1991Jul2.201853.3755@rodan.acs.syr.edu>, amichiel@rodan.acs.syr.edu (Allen J Michielsen) writes:

[Note: I think you wanted Followups-to: Poster, not your actual Email address]

>   I have a KDA50-QA that I need to reconfigure as controller DUBx:
> I have read the KDA50 user guide, and it gives the standard base address
> of 172150 and provides jumpers and switches for address lines 2-12, and
> documents possible floating addresses of 160334 & 160340 (all 3 in octal).
> While it is easy to reconfigure to either of these addresses (or any other
> address {unused} in that range), I can't seem to locate in either that manual
> or any of the VMS manuals, what the standard address / default address / 
> supported address / automatic address for device/controller DUBx: should be.
> I assume that it is 160334, but can anybody verify this ???

  You should always ask SYSGEN what it wants, for SYSGEN's rules are subtle
and quick to change 8-).

$ mcr sysgen
SYSGEN>  CONFIG
DEVICE> uda,2
DEVICE> ^Z
Device: UDA 	Name: PUA  	 CSR: 772150  	 Vector: 154  	Support: yes 
Device: UDA 	Name: PUB  	 CSR: 760334* 	 Vector: 300* 	Support: yes 
SYSGEN> ^Z

>    On a second note, I see that the vector for this card is selected in soft-
> ware, and not jumperable, settable via hardware.  The manual suggests that
> 154 is the expected vector when using address 172150, but doesn't give a clue
> what to expect for a alternate vector with a alternate address, being used
> as DUBx:.  Being the sort of system with lots of junk being installed, used,
> removed, and changed, I'd like to know what the standard vector to expect is.
> (That way, when doing options, I'd avoid that vector, and yes, I know full well
> this is neither required or how vectors work, but I want to know anyway...)

  Depending on what other hardware is in the system, SYSGEN will pick differ-
ent vectors. Normally, the 2nd through Nth UDA will use the first unassigned
vector above the last assigned vector, seperated by whatever gap the previous
device uses for vectors. In such cases, SYSGEN will always move the vector to
an unused position.

