Subject: Re: Hardware reliability of 5000/120's?
Message-ID: <MELLON.91Jul9130939@nigiri.pa.dec.com>
Date: 9 Jul 91 11:09:39 GMT
References: <DA.91Jul9120228@ollie.Brown.EDU>
Sender: news@pa.dec.com (News)
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Lines: 45
In-Reply-To: da@ollie.Brown.EDU's message of 9 Jul 91 16:02:28 GMT


It sounds like you may have a questionable supply of AC power to these
machines.  The power supplies will refuse to start if the incoming
120VAC doesn't look good (i.e., low or high voltage), and it's also
possible that if you have a SCSI device plugged into a different AC
circuit than the CPU, you might have trouble - current SCSI
implementations don't use differential signals, so they can be
susceptible to differences in the ground voltage on seperate circuits.

An interesting test would be to swap the CPU that's known to work with
one that's known to fail, and see if the failure moves or stays in the
same place.   If all the intermittant units are on the same AC
circuit, and the other unit is on a different circuit, then you've
probably found your problem.   Check that circuit for large inductive
loads which may be flaking you out - refrigerators, fans, electric
drills, and anything with an electric motor are pretty common causes
of trouble.

Another source of trouble is an overloaded power circuit.   If a
circuit is right on the edge, spinning up a disk (a small inductive
load) can cause a brief brownout which could scramble the brains of
one of the SCSI devices or the system unit.

You may also be running into a problem having to do with power cycling
the devices on a SCSI bus.   If you power cycle one of the devices on
a bus, including the CPU, but not some others, you can wind up with
the SCSI bus in a bizarre state that it won't be able to get out of
with a software reset.

The same thing can happen if you plug'n'play while things are powered
on, and it's even possible to power things on in an order which
results in a bizarre SCSI state, although I haven't seen this happen
regularly since the prototype days of the DECstation 3100, where we
had a capacitance problem on the SCSI adapter.

All of the above are pretty way-out situations, but you might want to
see if they do relate to your problems, just in case.   I have
encountered all of the above problems since I started dealing with
small computers (minis and mainframes usually sit in a machine room
with conditioned power).

Other than that, you'll just have to try to get Field Circus to fix
things, I suppose.   

			       _MelloN_


