Originator: pauld@stowe.cs.washington.edu

Before I begin, I should point out that I am aware that any
affirmative answer to this question will be highly non-portable, and very
compiler-implementation dependent. Please do not waste network
bandwidth to tell me or anyone else that what am I about to ask about
cannot be done portably - I already know that, and that's why I'm
posting.

I have so far come across two implementations of an nargs() function.
nargs() returns the number of arguments passed to the calling
function. Hence, 


	foo (int a, int b, int c)

	{
		printf ("%d\n", nargs())
	}

when called correctly will display "3".

Both the implementations are rooted in knowing a lot about the
compiler's calling conventions for the stack. They are also both for
CISC architectures (Sequent Symmetry 386 and Sun 3 (68030)).

Now I'm faced with porting some code which uses nargs() to a MIPS
system. The rest of the port is done, and there are other methods I
can think of to circumvent the need for nargs, but neverthless, outof
intellectual curiosity, I'm wondering how difficult it would be to
write such a function for a MIPS compiler ? I remember some stuff
about how RISC compilers make much greater use of registers and so
forth, but I know so little about this stuff, that I can't begin to
guess.

Would anyone like to comment ? E-mail would be preferred, but I'll
watch these groups for a few days.

- paul


