Hi, I had asked a while ago how to connect a non-Digital PostScript
printer to a DEC5000 and got many helpful responses - thank you to
all who responded.

The printer, connected to the second serial port of DEC5000, is
working fine now, using XON/XOFF handshaking. This works well in most
situations except (as I found out this past weekend) when some energy
conscious person turns the printer off. There is no handshaking in
this case and jobs appear to be printed normally (disappear from the
queue after a while) but the result is equivalent to dumping to
/dev/null instead of /dev/tty01.

My understanding (please correct me if I am wrong) is that with DTR
handshaking scheme my workstation will not be able to write to the serial
port when the printer is off or disconnected, and therefore will leave
the print job on the the print queue.

My printer is capable of DTR handshaking and the printer cable I am using
connects all the required pins, but I can't figure out how to force the
workstation to use this method instead of XON/XOFF. I thought it has
to be in printcap with  "fs"/"xs" but I can't figure out how. If I switch
the printer to use DTR handshaking then they don't handshake at all.

Is there a way to make DEC5000 to use DTR handshaking? If so, how?

I have DEC5000 running Ultrix 4.1; here's the printcap entry:
lp|lp0|0|ps|Abaton:
	:af=/usr/adm/lpacct:\
