Seeing comments on the gray wall reminded me to put together this list
of complaints.  No flames intended, just some places where things could
be done better.

Every upgrade comes with a few manuals that supercede others, but it's
not easily possible to tell what they replace.  For example, I had to
open and scan thru the "Guide To Installing Ultrix" to find that it
supercedes both "Basic Installation" and "Advanced Installation".
Similar comments can be made about most of the other docs.  In the
front of each manual that came with 4.0 it says "applies to Ultrix 4.0
and later".  But some have been superceded, so this is no longer true.
It's a misleading statement.  I think they should drop the "and later"
line.  They should also include a set of instructions like "this box of
new 4.2 manuals replaces such-and-such manuals from the 4.1 set and
such-and-such from the 4.0 set."

Now we've got a mix of 4.0, 4.1 and 4.2 docs that's getting more and
more haphazard.  Every upgrade introduces possibility of error, either
mine or decs.  DEC should ship a list of current manual numbers with
each OS upgrade.  Now I can just look at the 'AA-PBKXB-TE' on the
bottom of "Guide To X/Open Transport Interface" and know that it's
current.  Similarly, if I find a copy of the same doc in some departed
persons cube, 'AA-PBKXA-TE' tells me it's outdated.  A simple
cross-product sheet (ok, sheets) would make life much easier.

DEC should tell you when if manuals are new.  A pile of Display
Postscript manuals came with 4.1.  Were they brand new, or did they
replace some 4.0 docs that I've just misplaced?

Caveat -- this is not a flame on DEC.  I appreciate their efforts in
putting together the gray way.  These are just a few problems noted
while assembling things for a 4.2 install and suggestions for
improvement.
