In article <45956@netnews.upenn.edu> chaim@eniac.seas.upenn.edu (Chaim Dworkin) writes:
>I can probably guess the answer to this one but I'll ask anyway.....
>What's the difference between a microPDP-11 and a Pro-350/Pro-380?
>
>My first guess would be that the microPDP-11 has none of the limitations
>of the Pro.  But then I've heard that the limitations put into the pro
>were to prevent the Pro from taking away from the PDP-11 market.  If that
>is so then the micro PDP-11 would also take away from the market for
>full size PDP-11s.
>
>chaim
>
>--
>Chaim Dworkin				   (chaim@eniac.seas.upenn.edu)

The MicroPDP-11 certainly did take away from the market for full size
PDP-11's, thanks to the J-11 chip!

A MicroPDP-11 is a Q-Bus system with one of the following processors:
    11/23+, 11/53, 11/53+, 11/73, 11/83, 11/93

All of these but the 11/23+ are based on the J-11 chipset and differ in
things like clock rate and memory type.  All can run any of the standard
PDP-11 operating systems and support all the various Q-Bus options.

The PRO-350 is an 11/23+ CPU, while the PRO-380 is an 11/73.  The 380
has better graphics and supports more memory.  Apart from that, they're
very similar:  they use a special system bus (CTI, I think) that's used
in nothing else.  They don't support Q-Bus options, alas.  Software-wise,
they support P/OS (a [mostly] single-user implementation of RSX-11M+),
RT-11, and maybe a third-party UNIX or two.  One notable problem with
the PRO is that the bitmapped screen is handled in software by the CPU,
which slows it down quite a bit.

A PRO-380 can be an interesting home machine for people who like PDP-11's
and RSX, but a real PDP-11 has fewer limitations.  Get the real thing if
you can afford it.  Software is the biggest cost issue, really.  Most PRO
software is free; Digital released it to DECUS.  Not so with the PDP-11
stuff.

