The following request is forwarded to this group for a friend of mine, who
doesn't have access to this group on internet. 
Please send any response directly to him, rather  than me.

-------------------------------------------

Hi,

I am a graduate student at the EE dept of Univ of  Rhode  Island.
Together  with my advisor, I am doing research in designing cache
hierachy to speedup some  signal processing programs on a  super-
scalar  uniprocessor  computer.  In  order  to  verify our design
ideas, we  plan to do a  trace-driven simulation.

We have already obtained a two-level cache  simulator  from   the
public domain in Stanford Univ. We even have the traces generated
after the executions of the programs instrumented by  a  software
called  "pixie",  which  is  available on the  DEC MIPS stations.
However, the  trace file is not a text file,  thus  we     cannot  
access it using the regular unix I/O instructions without knowing 
the file format. According to the  on-line  manual   descriptions   
on  pixie and related commands like "pixstats" and "prof",    the 
trace file is supposed to  be used for execution profile.

We have asked for help from everybody who could offer support  in
this  university, but none of them has any idea about pixie. Even
worse, there is no hardcopy manual for this pixie on campus.   In
fact,  we  do know some recent study reported in this year's  ACM
Symposium on Comp Archi  did use the trace  generated  via  pixie
for  trace driven simulation study on cache performance.  Because
we  haven't  got  those  researchers'  e-mail  address  or  phone
number, and we assume  maybe many people on the Internet know how
to read the "pixied" trace, I thus forward this request of help.

What we need in short is the file specification and relevant sub-
routines to access the trace file. Besides reading each item from
the trace, we also hope to know how to identify    the associated
instruction (or  address for load or store) and data type   (word
or double word). Since "prof" must also use the above information
we figure these subroutines or data type specification  must   be
existing, though without you guy's hint we don't know how  to use 
yet.

Looking forward to whatever responses  from  your  guys  in  this
group, I highly appreciate whatever help you  will offer to us.



hong wang

