> difficult is it going to be to keep one of these machines going ?

Optimisitically, uninstalled means that a manufacturers service representative
has come in and neatly unplugged all the cables and bundled everything up.
Pessimistically, it means that the system is no longer operational and all
the cables and miscellaneous bits have been tossed.  Make sure you pick up
any console boot/diagnostic tapes, print sets and "software" you can with
each system.

The best advice is to find a hardware junkie who is familar with 7XX systems
or a service person willing to do a little moonlighting to help you get it
working.  A 750 really isn't that hard to set up, but there are a few less
than obvious things you could waste a lot of time on...

A 750 can be a pretty nice machine from the point of "keeping it going" -
we have one here that has had *no* CPU maintenance over the last 5 years
and still works (well, it crashes every few months).  Some disks, RA80's
and RM05's will last seemingly forever (until they break 8-).  Power
consumption is low, at least in comparison to a 780.

On the downside, you've got .7 MIPS and 8 M-byte of memory.  Unless you're
really commited to having a pet 750, any 386 PC clone will run rings around
it, and a MicroVAX II or Vaxstation will probably cost you less in the long
run, in terms of operating expenses and disk/tape repairs/expansion.

Have fun.

