Xref: gmdzi comp.sys.dec:24834 comp.unix.ultrix:46858

The logical structure of SCSI disks doesn't closely represent the physical 
sector layout. There are typically 1 to 3 alternate sectors per cylinder and 
1 or 2 reserved cylinders, in case there are more than 3 bad sectors in any 
cylinder.

[Ultrix lets you display these parameters with the command:
 'rzdisk -g current /dev/rrz0c' 
			^-- insert appropriate device, use partition 'c'!]

This is the reason for some other problems, not only does Ultrix loose blocks 
by trying to align partitions to cylinder boundaries, but in fact the resulting 
file systems are (nearly) guaranteed not to begin or end on a cylinder 
boundary. Ultrix calculates sectors per cylinder by multiplying sectors by the 
number of heads, but without subtracting the alternate sectors...
That makes it look like you can't access the inner 20 tracks of the drive.

There are some more bad things that happen because of the wrong idea the 
kernel has about the disk layout. These confuse the optimisation heuristics 
of the Berkeley Fast File System. The result is a lot of head movement 
between adjacent cylinders. The file system tries to put adjacent file blocks 
into one cylinder as long as possible. But because of the alternate blocks, 
the cylinder boundary is at another place than the kernel expects it to be.
So, the kernel put the blocks in the upper half of one and the lower of the 
other cylinder. 

It would be nice if DEC did something about this...

Stefan
