Charlie,

|>
|>I am trying to do unattended backup too, but no luck yet.
|>From the manual, it seems saying any user activity could corrupt the
|>backup, so is it necessary to turn the machine to single user mode before
|>performing backup?

This bit of your question has be talked about before, but here is a quick resume.
Dump first scans the file system to be dumped and from the information it picks up 
it decides what to put to tape. If the filesystem is active i.e. being written
the data found during the scan pass will not be correct, and hence files could
be corrupted.
If no writes occur between the scan and the dumping of the filesystem No Problem.


|>I am having a RZ57 1GB disk and a TLZ04 1.2 GB DAT Tape drive on our
|>DECstation 5000.  And I have a rz0a (/) 20MB and rz0g (/usr) 900MB partitions.
|>Can I put them both on one tape?  I used "dump 0uf /dev/rmt1h /usr" for the
|>partition rz0g.  When I have used just a small part on the tape, how can I
|>utilize the other part?

If you use the non-rewind device for the dumps the tape stays at the end of the
previous dump.
		e.g. dump 0uf /dev/nrmt0h /usr ; dump 0uf /dev/rmt0h /

This would dump /usr then / and finally rewind tape.

You can use the mt command to space forward over dumps already written on tape.


|>When I do incremental backup, it seems to do the job all over. Where it
|>finds the last full save backup's info?  Do I need the previous tape?

It reads /etc/dumpdates file for info.( the u flag to dump causes this file
to be updated).

|>Can I use the same tape?  Would that overwrite everything on the tape?

Providing you get to the end of any prevoiusly written dumps using "mt" command.

|>How can I erase a tape?

Not sure on that one.

|>Is it possible to use "tar" to complete a file system backup?

Not really sure what you mean. A tar archivbe can be produced for a complete 
partition as it is recursive.
Not as good as dump though ( Not sure about sym links etc).


