My department, Homewood Academic Computing of The Johns Hopkins University, 
is replacing its current Unix timesharing system (an AT&T 3b4000) with a new
Unix "server" system.  We serve students and faculty doing a mix of (mostly) 
instructional and (some) research computing. Thus, it is more important for 
us to have a strong multi-user machine than one that will run a few huge 
programs. Our intent is to provide campus-wide compute and file service 
while continuing to provide some timesharing. The vendors currently being 
considered are: DEC, Sun, IBM, HP, Silicon Graphics, and AT&T. 

I was asked to communicate with other organizations (Universities especially)
who have gone through a similar process or are installing a "server" system
as their first central Unix system. Of particular interest are issues such as:

-Does your system perform "as advertised"? 
-Did the vendor give (and continue to give) you the support you needed?
-Were you able to port your applications and programs, and with what
 degree of difficulty?
-Do you do resource accounting and billing, and if so how did you
 implement it?
-Do you run an X-Windows client on your system, and if so what has been 
 your experience with it (especially network impact)? 
-What are the things you didn't think of and wish you had, and what
 didn't the vendor tell you about (or gloss over)?

Any general comments about machines, operating systems, the transition
process, running distributed applications (particularly databases) and so
on would be greatly appreciated.  I would be particularly interested in 
hearing from anyone with one of the newer high-performance Unix platforms,
such as the server versions of the IBM RS/6000 or HP 9000 model 800 series.

Please reply via e-mail, or better yet send your phone number and I'll call 
you--it'll save you time and money and I'll get a better understanding of 
your experience!

Thanks for any insight you can provide.

